2015 was a banner year for privacy enforcement actions. The California Attorney General's (AG) Office fined four businesses a total of $225,000 for privacy violations under the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA). Specifically, three companies were fined for failing to disclose whether or not they sold personal information; one company was fined for failing to allow consumers to opt out of the sale of their personal information; and one company was fined for failing to notify consumers about the sale of their personal information. In addition, in March 2015, the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) took action against LabMD, a Georgia-based medical diagnostics company, for allegedly failing to adequately secure consumers' sensitive personal data. The FTC's complaint alleges that LabMD did not use reasonable and appropriate security measures to protect sensitive consumer information, including consumers' health information and Social Security numbers.
Q: Are employers required to have a written privacy policy that explains their collection and use of data?
A: Yes, under the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) Act, and in some cases, under the California Investigative Consumer Reporting Agencies Act (ICRAA), businesses are required to have a privacy policy that informs consumers of the categories of personal information that the business collects, the purposes for which the business collects and shares such personal information, and the specific business or commercial purpose for which the business collects personal information.
Q: What should a privacy policy include?
A: California's CCPA and the FTC's rules provide comprehensive lists of information that should be included in a privacy policy. A privacy policy should include, at minimum, the following information:
The categories of personal information the business collects;
The categories of third parties, if any, with whom the business shares the personal information;
The specific purposes for which the business collects and shares personal information;
The consumers' rights to exercise their rights under the CCPA, including information about how to exercise those rights;
Information about security and integrity of the personal information collected;
The methods by which the business will respond to consumer requests, including information about how to exercise the right to access their personal information and obtain it from the business;
Notices about the business's actions in response to a consumer's exercise of the right to access their personal information, including information about what the business will do with a consumer's request and whether the business will retain the personal information or delete it;
Information about the commercial purposes of the business's sale of the personal information, including the categories of the personal information that the business collects for a commercial purpose, and the categories of third parties, if any, with whom the business shares the personal information;
Notice about the right to opt-out of the business's sale of the consumer's personal information, including information about the process for the consumer to opt-out;
Notice about the right to equal service and price, even if a consumer exercises their right to opt-out;
The California Attorney General's Office has provided a sample form that can be used to create a privacy policy, which is available at: https://oag.ca.gov/privacy/ccpa/ccpa-sample-notice.
Q: What should a privacy policy include to ensure compliance with the GDPR?
A: The GDPR requires businesses to include a number of details in a privacy policy, including:
Information about the data controller and contact details;
Information about the purposes of processing personal information;
Details of a data protection officer, if applicable;
Information about the retention period for personal information;
Information about the rights of the data subject, including: (i) the right of access, (ii) the right to rectification, (iii) the right to erasure, (iv) the right to restriction, (v) the right to data portability, (vi) the right to object, and (vii) the right to be informed about a personal data breach;
Information about the legal basis for the processing of the personal information, which may be based on consent, performance of a contract, compliance with a legal obligation, or the legitimate interests of the controller or a third party;
Information about the data protection officer;
Information about the data protection officer's contact details; and
Information about how to lodge a complaint with the supervisory authority.
